Let $I_{\Lambda} = n_{\infty} + \lambda n_{0}$ with $\lambda = \Lambda/6$ be the frozen point of Lemma \claimref{mass.de-sitter-scale-frozen-point}, and $Z^{1}, Z^{2} \in \CS$ two conformal spinors (twistors).
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(The deformed bracket.)} The pairing of the composite against the frozen point is
\begin{equation}
B_{\Lambda} := \ip{I_{\Lambda}}{Z^{1}\wedge Z^{2}} = \br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}} + \frac{\Lambda}{6}\,[\omega^{1}\omega^{2}],
\end{equation}
and at $\Lambda = 0$ it is the classical contraction of the composite with the point at infinity $n_{\infty}$, read as a bivector of $\CS$ (Penrose's infinity twistor).
\item \emph{(The mass.)} The grip $P = M(I_{\Lambda})$ has squared length
\begin{equation}
m^{2} = Q(P) = 2\,|B_{\Lambda}|^{2} + Q(I_{\Lambda})\, C = 2\,|B_{\Lambda}|^{2} + \frac{\Lambda}{3}\, C .
\end{equation}
\item \emph{(The flat shadow.)} At $\Lambda = 0$, $m^{2} = 2|\ip{I}{Z^{1}\wedge Z^{2}}|^{2} = 2|\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}|^{2}$: Proposition \claimref{mass.mass-symplectic-area-two-twistor-formula}, read as the squared pairing of the composite against the frozen point.
\item \emph{(The point-supported composite.)} If both spinors lie on the line $L_{x}$ of the point $x$, the composite bivector is $\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}X(x)$ and $B_{\Lambda} = \sigma(x)\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}} = \bigl(1 - \tfrac{\Lambda Q(x)}{12}\bigr)\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}$: the flat bracket read in the de Sitter scale.
\item \emph{(The massless locus.)} For $\Lambda > 0$, $m^{2} \ge 0$, and $m^{2} = 0$ if and only if $B_{\Lambda} = 0$ and $h$ is a multiple of the identity; that multiple is zero, so the condition is $B_{\Lambda} = 0$ and $h = 0$ (\claimref{synthesis.many-twistor-join}(v)). The doubled composite $Z^{2} = Z^{1}$ has $B_{\Lambda} = 0$ and $m^{2} = \tfrac{\Lambda}{3}\,(\bar{Z}\cdot Z)^{2}$, so it is massless only when its constituent is null; and there are massless composites that are not doubled, for instance $Z^{1} = e_{1} + e_{4}$, $Z^{2} = \alpha e_{1} + \beta e_{4}$ with $\alpha \neq \beta$.
\end{enumerate}

On the real slice, where $\varepsilon^{A'B'}$ is the conjugate of $\varepsilon^{AB}$ and $\bar\pi$ the conjugate of $\pi$ (the conjugation reverses the complex unit $I_{6}$): a single conformal spinor (twistor) carries null momentum $p_{AA'} = \pi_{A}\bar\pi_{A'}$ and is massless, and a system of two conformal spinors with momentum
\[
p_{AA'} = \pi^{1}_{A}\bar\pi^{1}_{A'} + \pi^{2}_{A}\bar\pi^{2}_{A'}
\]
has
\begin{equation}
m^{2} = p^{2} = 2\,\bigl|\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}\bigr|^{2},
\end{equation}
the squared symplectic area between the two spinor parts. Aligned spinors (vanishing bracket) give a null composite, and mass grows with the misalignment.

Let $\Lambda > 0$. Define the \emph{induced weight} of a composite of mass $m$ as the weight of a density with that mass under the dictionary, that is, a solution $w$ of $-w(w+3)H^{2} = m^{2}$. For the composite of two conformal spinors with $m^{2}$ given by \claimref{mass.exact-join}:
\begin{equation}
w = -\frac32 \pm i\nu, \qquad \nu^{2} = \frac{6\,|B_{\Lambda}|^{2}}{\Lambda} + C - \frac94,
\end{equation}
the weight being real exactly when $6|B_{\Lambda}|^{2}/\Lambda + C \le \tfrac94$, and otherwise on the principal series with $\nu = m/H - \tfrac98 H/m + O((H/m)^{3})$. The real part is $-\tfrac32$ for every composite heavier than $3H/2$; the mass lies in the imaginary part, and for $|B_{\Lambda}| \gg H\sqrt{C}$, which is the regime of every known particle when $C$ is of order one, $\mathrm{Im}\,w = \sqrt{2}\,|B_{\Lambda}|/H$ at leading order.

Under Hypothesis (S), let the electroweak vev be a vector $x$ of the ten (a rung vector, $Y = \mp	frac12$), and let the three families be carried by a tensor factor $\C^{3}$ on which the family structure acts by an arbitrary matrix $F$, so that the mass matrices are $M_{e} = y_{e}\,F$ and $M_{d} = y_{d}\,F$ with $y_{e} = Y_{x}(L, e^{c})$, $y_{d} = Y_{x}(Q, d^{c})$ the one-family Clebsches. Then $|y_{e}| = |y_{d}|$, so $M_{e}$ and $M_{d}$ have identical characteristic polynomials up to sign, hence identical spectra in magnitude, for every $F$: no operation in family space, the breaking $S_{3} 	o S_{2} 	o 1$ included, produces $m_{\mu} 
eq m_{s}$ or $m_{e} 
eq m_{d}$ at tree level. The same holds for $M_{
u}^{D}$ against $M_{u}$.

Under $S(U(2) \times U(3))$, with $Y$ the hypercharge just defined:
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Unconditional.) The pair-level generators, the cells of the tensor state space, transform as $(2, 3)$ with abelian weight $q = 1$, so $Y = 1/6$: the quark doublet's representation. A single pair has one unit of hypercharge.
\item (Under Hypothesis (S).) The states with one slot mode occupied and the pair modes in an $SU(3)$-invariant configuration are $\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{0}W$, weight $3$ ($Y = +1/2$, total mode number one, odd), and $\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{3}W$, weight $-3$ ($Y = -1/2$, total mode number four, even); both transform as $(2, 1)$. Without (S) neither exists in the state space, whose only row-filling states form the quadruplet $(4,1)$ of weight $+3$.
\item (Unconditional as a computation on the Fock space of a single conformal spinor, with the pair-slot factor acting alone; that Fock space is not a subspace of the tensor state space, and under (S) it is the row $j = 0$.) In the sector of a single conformal spinor (the single-twistor sector) $\Lambda^{\bullet}W = \Lambda^{\bullet}\C^{3}$, the grade-$k$ states transform as $\Lambda^{k}\C^{3}$, that is as $1, 3, \bar{3}, 1$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, 3$, with weight $q = -2k$: $Y = 0, -1/3, -2/3, -1$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, under (S), the fermion content of the composite at the particle handedness (Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency}) is
\begin{equation}
(2,3)_{1/6} \oplus (2,1)_{-1/2},
\end{equation}
quark doublet plus lepton doublet, with colour neutrality of the lepton the statement that a state with its pair modes full is an $SU(3)$ singlet: a lepton is a complete conformal spinor, one slot with the pair system full, while a quark is one slot with one pair.
